Git primarily fits in the "Develop" phase but also supports Build, Test, Release, and even Operate via GitOps and automation pipelines.

⚙️ Real-Time Tasks DevOps Engineers Do with Git:
•Branching Strategy- Implementing Git workflows like Git Flow, Trunk-Based, or Feature Branch workflows.   
•Code Integration- Merging code from feature branches to main or develop after code review and approval.   
•CI/CD Triggers- Configuring pipelines (Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, etc.) to trigger on git push, PR, or tag.   
•Access Control- Managing repository permissions, enforcing branch protection rules.   
•Audit & Compliance- Reviewing Git history, commit logs, and who made what changes.   
•Rollbacks- Using Git commands (revert, reset, checkout) to undo breaking changes.   
•Infra-as-Code- Versioning Storing Terraform/Ansible/Kubernetes manifests in Git and managing versions.   
•GitOps- Using Git as a single source of truth to deploy Kubernetes resources via tools like ArgoCD or Flux.  

🧰 Example 1: CI/CD with Jenkins & GitHub
Dev pushes code → GitHub triggers webhook → Jenkins builds & tests → If success, deploys to dev environment.
🌐 Example 2: GitOps with ArgoCD
DevOps updates a Kubernetes YAML file in Git → ArgoCD detects change → Syncs to Kubernetes cluster.
🔐 Example 3: Access Control & Auditing
DevOps enforces GitHub branch protection → Only reviewers can approve PRs(Pull Request) → All changes are audited.
